00:33:18,929 --> 00:33:23,034
So there are certain types of models also that are not autoregressive. But all

502
00:33:23,082 --> 00:33:26,403
the models that you use right now, that is the industry standard in language

503
00:33:26,468 --> 00:33:30,559
processing, are autoregressive in nature. And what we mean by autoregressive in

504
00:33:31,059 --> 00:33:33,688
this case is that the model actually takes in a sequence of input,

505
00:33:35,132 --> 00:33:38,951
sequence of tokens as input, which is a prefix of when

506
00:33:38,983 --> 00:33:42,337
we train it. These models are trained with certain prefix and they are designed

507
00:33:42,837 --> 00:33:46,108
to train what comes after. And in this case, when we are doing a test

508
00:33:46,608 --> 00:33:49,558
time, it could be your prompt or your input or your question. And the model

509
00:33:50,058 --> 00:33:53,993
has to actually complete this by generating the remaining part

510
00:33:54,493 --> 00:33:57,978
of the sequence. And the way it's usually done is that we have this input

511
00:33:58,090 --> 00:34:02,011
that we provide to the model. And at every time step, what the model

512
00:34:02,511 --> 00:34:05,433
can do is looking at the input that it has seen so far,

513
00:34:05,883 --> 00:34:08,936
its goal is to actually generate an output, which let's call it

514
00:34:09,436 --> 00:34:12,679
yt. And what happens is that the autoregressive

515
00:34:12,712 --> 00:34:16,292
part comes like this. We have seen this diagram also Previously, when we were talking

516
00:34:16,324 --> 00:34:20,203
about— we have seen this diagram multiple times in the previous

