00:39:04,671 --> 00:39:08,542
decoding, let's try to understand what is what is it

591
00:39:08,574 --> 00:39:12,168
that we're trying to actually do when we are training a model, right? So again,

592
00:39:12,810 --> 00:39:16,355
we have seen these recipes independently for

593
00:39:16,435 --> 00:39:20,414
different specific architectures. Today what we're trying to do is we are trying to actually

594
00:39:20,446 --> 00:39:24,056
formalize the more abstract concept so that we

595
00:39:24,556 --> 00:39:28,436
talk about it from a model-agnostic way, right? So the whole goal again is

596
00:39:28,452 --> 00:39:33,168
to actually minimize the negative log likelihood of predicting

597
00:39:33,201 --> 00:39:36,652
the next tokens in the sequence, right? Or the maximum likelihood objective,

598
00:39:36,668 --> 00:39:39,767
as I said previously. And we discussed this also when we were talking about word

599
00:39:40,267 --> 00:39:43,958
embeddings, also about language modeling. Eventually, this boils down to a multi-class classification task.

600
00:39:44,103 --> 00:39:47,715
Why? Because we want to actually choose the correct

601
00:39:48,215 --> 00:39:51,986
token, which is again a class which is defined over all possible tokens

602
00:39:52,486 --> 00:39:55,583
in the vocabulary. And each token in the vocabulary is a class label, right?

603
00:39:55,631 --> 00:39:59,019
So again, what you see is that this becomes like a multi-class classification problem.

604
00:39:59,356 --> 00:40:02,886
And what you know is that you already know what is the correct

605
00:40:03,386 --> 00:40:06,817
token at timestep t, which is what we also refer to

